<h2>I'm Blair Wilson</h2>
<p>I'm not a web designer. I'm an operator. My background is multi-site retail and hospitality — McDonald's, Mitre 10, Z Energy — and I founded and ran Wilson Barbecue.</p>
<p>That matters more than it sounds. Those are all businesses that live or die on one thing: whether the person standing in front of you decides to buy. Not on design awards. Not on brand decks. On whether the offer is clear, the trust is there, and the next step is obvious.</p>
<p>That's the same problem your website has to solve. It's why I built WFT the way I did.</p>

<h2>Why I only build for tradies</h2>
<p>Most web agencies will build anything for anyone. A café this week, a law firm the next, a plumber after that. Every project starts from scratch, which is why they take six weeks and cost five grand.</p>
<p>I only build for trades. That means I already know what a sparkie's customer types into Google at 8pm with no power, why a builder's enquiry form needs different fields to a drainlayer's, and which suburb pages actually pull work rather than just existing.</p>
<p>Narrow is what makes fast and cheap possible without cutting corners. It's also what makes the guarantee possible — you can only underwrite a risk you can calculate.</p>

<h2>Why there's a guarantee no one else offers</h2>
<p>Because a promise that costs nothing to make is worth nothing.</p>
<p>Every agency in the country says they'll get you results. None of them put anything behind it. I'll rebuild your site free if it doesn't produce a genuine job enquiry in {{ site.guaranteeDays }} days, and credit you NZ$200 if I miss the {{ site.buildDays }} deadline. Both sets of <a href="/guarantee/">conditions are published in full</a>, because a guarantee with hidden fine print is marketing, not a guarantee.</p>

<h2>I'm new at this, and I'm not going to pretend otherwise</h2>
<p>WFT launched recently. There are no five-star testimonials on this site because I haven't earned them yet, and inventing them would insult the intelligence of exactly the people I want to work with — tradies have seen every kind of dodgy operator and can smell one at range.</p>
<p>So instead of fake proof, you get the deck stacked in your favour: both guarantees in writing, and my full attention on a small number of builds each month. In return, once the site is live and bringing in work, you give me an honest review. If it doesn't work, you say that instead.</p>
<p>That's the trade. It's a better deal for you than it is for me, and it's how a new business earns a track record without lying about having one.</p>

<h2>How I think about your website</h2>
<p>A website is not a brochure and it's not a piece of art. It's a salesperson that works while you're under a house. It has one job: turn someone who's never heard of you into someone who calls you.</p>
<p>Which means the things that matter are the unglamorous ones — does it load fast on a phone with two bars, does it say what you do and where, does it look like a real business, is the phone number impossible to miss, does Google know you exist. That's what {{ site.priceDisplay }} buys. Not a redesign of your logo.</p>

<h2>What I won't do</h2>
<ul>
  <li><strong>Lock you in.</strong> You own the site and the domain outright. Leave whenever you like and I'll hand over the files.</li>
  <li><strong>Discount.</strong> The price is the same for client one as client one hundred — a price that moves when you push was padded to start with. If the budget genuinely doesn't stretch, we'd rather trim the scope honestly than fake a markdown.</li>
  <li><strong>Take work I'm wrong for.</strong> Need e-commerce, a booking platform or a custom web app? A full agency is genuinely the right call, and I'll tell you that on the call rather than take your money.</li>
  <li><strong>Promise a #1 ranking.</strong> Nobody can guarantee that. Anyone who does is either lying or about to redefine "#1" for you.</li>
</ul>

